  "worked_solution": "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{\u2032}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})+8(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ (8\\alpha +8\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(12\\beta +8\\beta ){e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 16\\alpha {e}^{2x}+20\\beta {e}^{3x} & =5{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n16\\alpha =5,\\,\\,20\\beta =3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{5}{16},\\,\\,\\beta =\\frac{3}{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{16}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{3}{20}{e}^{3x}.\n$$",
